The following information may be useful when altering the GS/OS graphics splashscreen.The splash screen is generated by GLoader by storing graphics data directly to thesuper-hi-res display buffer.  The icon and text strings are stored as bitmaps withinGLoader.  To modify any of these items, it is necessary to alter the bitmaps thatrepresent them.To modify the text strings, take the following steps:1)  Transport the Splash.Screen and MakeSource files to an Apple IIGS.2)	Use AppleWorks.GS to edit this file.  The file is an AppleWorks.GS graphics	file, which means that the text can be manipulated as objects.	3)	Make the necessary changes, then save the file for any possible future editing.4)	Use the "Save AsÉ" menu item to save the image as an Apple Preferred Format	graphic file.  This is necessary to save the text "objects" as bitmap images.5)	Close the document, then open the Apple Preferred Format file that was just	created.6)	Use the rectangular selection tool to select the new text string.  Try to keep	the selection as small as possible.7)	Once the selection is complete, use the "Copy" menu item (or use Command-C)	to copy the selection to the clipboard.8)	Exit AppleWorks.GS (there's no need to save anything at this point).  AWGS should	display a message telling you that it's saving the clipboard.9)	Launch APW and execute the MakeSource utility.  The syntax is:		MakeSource label >file	The "label" argument will be used to generate the labels that are used by	GLoader when it is assembled.  It should be either "text1" or "text2".  The	"file" argument is the file which will receive the redirected output from the	utility.		This utility will read the clipboard file and generate the source text	required for inclusion in the build of GLoader.10)	Port the output file from MakeSoure back to MPW and cut-and-paste as necessary	into the GLoader source file.To modify the icon, use an Apple IIGS icon editor (such as DIcEd) that has thecapability to save the icon data as source text.  Once the source has been produced,port the file to the GLoader source.NOTE:  GLoader does not require the iconType, iconSize, or iconMask fields of thegenerated icon record.  These fields must be deleted from GLoader's icon definitionprior to assembly.Greg BrancheApple II System Software24 September 1990